WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 166: Woodrow Wilson and the Fourteen Points: The Paris Peace Conference and the League (1918–1919)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.320
[Rebecca] Today we step into the Gilded Hall of Mirrors at the Palace of Versailles alongside Woodrow Wilson.

4
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:23.280
[Rebecca] In 1919, the American president sat at the pinnacle of global authority, attempting to forge a new international world order based on moral law rather than imperial conquest.

5
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:29.280
[Rebecca] In the Crucible of Paris, Wilson wrestled with European cynicism to give birth to the League of Nations.

6
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.240
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story.

7
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.400
[George] Wilson articulated his revolutionary 14 points.

8
00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:10.480
[George] The program called for open covenants of peace openly arrived at, absolute freedom of the seas, the removal of economic barriers, worldwide disarmament, and the impartial adjustment of all colonial claims based on the principle of national self-determination.

9
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:16.240
[George] Backslash and backslash and crowning the entire structure was the historic fourteenth point.

10
00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:28.080
[George] A general association of nations must be formed under specific covenants for the purpose of affording mutual guarantees of political independence and territorial integrity to great and small states alike.

11
00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:34.320
[George] Millions of Allied leaflets containing the 14 points were dropped by airplane behind German lines.

12
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.240
[George] The promise of a just, equitable peace without revenge broke the German army's will to fight.

13
00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:53.280
[George] When Germany signed the armistice on November 11, 1918, ending four years of slaughter, it surrendered with the explicit understanding that the peace treaty would be based upon Woodrow Wilson's fourteen points.

14
00:01:53.280 --> 00:02:06.680
[George] Wilson's fourteen points represented an audacious attempt to replace secret diplomacy, imperial conquests, and balance of power politics with open covenants, national self-determination, and collective security.

15
00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:18.440
[George] The Fourteen Points stood as an immortal beacon of hope, articulating the revolutionary principle that small nations possess the same right to self-determination as the great empires of the earth.

16
00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.680
[George] In December 1918, Wilson made a momentous decision.

17
00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:30.120
[George] He would lead the American peace delegation in person, becoming the first sitting American president ever to travel to Europe.

18
00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:40.840
[George] Steaming into the harbor of Brest, France, aboard the luxury liner George Washington, Wilson was greeted with religious adulation never before witnessed in human history.

19
00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:51.240
[George] Two million French citizens packed the champselissés in Paris, weeping and throwing bouquets of violets and roses at his carriage, chanting, Vive Wilson.

20
00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:51.880
[George] Vive L.

21
00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:52.040
[George] M.

22
00:02:52.200 --> 00:03:01.320
[George] Eric, backslash n backslash n in London, King George V hosted him at Buckingham Palace while tens of thousands of working people sang in the streets.

23
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:08.520
[George] In Rome, peasants knelt before portraits of Wilson adorned with candles, praying to him as if he were a Christian saint.

24
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:16.920
[George] To a wartorn continent that had buried twenty million sons, the austere American professor represented the moral redemption of humanity.

25
00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.320
[George] But behind the glittering receptions lay a harsh geopolitical reality.

26
00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:27.480
[George] European statesmen viewed Wilson's idealistic sermons with deep skepticism and cynical amusement.

27
00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:40.040
[George] The thunderous, emotional welcome accorded to Wilson in Paris, London, and Rome reflected the desperate hope of an exhausted European continent that American idealism could heal its generational wounds.

28
00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:50.800
[George] In the ruins of post-war Europe, Wilson was greeted not merely as an American president, but as an almost messianic savior who had come to liberate humanity from the scourge of war.

29
00:03:51.040 --> 00:04:10.160
[George] When the Paris Peace Conference opened in January 1919, real power consolidated into the hands of the Big Four Woodrow Wilson of the United States, Prime Minister David Lloyd George of Great Britain, Premier Georges Clemenceau of France, and Prime Minister Vittorio Orlando of Italy.

30
00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:16.480
[George] The meetings took place behind closed doors in Clemenceau's private study at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.

31
00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:22.320
[George] The cultural and ideological clash between Wilson and Clemenceau was immediate and ferocious.

32
00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:35.760
[George] Backslash N backslash N Georges Clemenceau, known as the Tiger, was a 77-year-old veteran of the Franco-Prussian War who wore grey silk gloves to cover eczema and carried two German bullet scars in his body.

33
00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.040
[George] France had lost 1.4 million soldiers.

34
00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.960
[George] Northeastern France was a moonscape of flooded mines, ruined cities, and poisoned soil.

35
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.200
[George] Clemenceau had zero interest in abstract international leagues.

36
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:55.840
[George] He wanted Germany dismembered, disarmed, and crushed so severely that it could never threaten France again.

37
00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:00.400
[George] Clemenceau mocked Wilson's moral certainty with biting French wit.

38
00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.600
[George] God gave us the Ten Commandments, and we broke them.

39
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.400
[George] Wilson has given us the fourteen points.

40
00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:07.680
[George] We shall see.

41
00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:18.880
[George] The grueling negotiations at the Qui Dorsey pitted Wilson's high minded vision against the bitter, vengeful realities of European leaders who had sacrificed millions of young men in the trenches.

42
00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] Clemenceau's cynical quip that God gave us the Ten Commandments and we broke them, Wilson gives us fourteen points, we shall see illustrated the profound philosophical divide at the peace table.

43
00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:36.920
[George] For five grueling months, Wilson fought a daily psychological war against his European allies.

44
00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:45.240
[George] Orlando demanded Austrian territory promised to Italy in secret treaties, walking out of the conference in tears when Wilson refused.

45
00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:51.000
[George] Clemenceau demanded that France annex the German Rhineland and the Coal Rich Tsar Basin.

46
00:05:51.000 --> 00:06:08.200
[George] When Wilson threatened to pack his bags and return to America aboard the George Washington, Clemenceau accused him of being pro-German, backslash n backslash n to save his beloved League of Nations, Wilson was forced into agonizing moral concessions that compromised his fourteen points.

47
00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:15.160
[George] He permitted Great Britain and France to seize Germany's African and Pacific colonies under the guise of League mandates.

48
00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:20.600
[George] He conceded Germany's Shandong province to Japan over the desperate protests of China.

49
00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:36.920
[George] Most disastrously, Wilson yielded to Allied demands that Germany accept full moral responsibility for the war under Article 231, the infamous war guilt clause, and agreed to pay astronomical financial reparations of $33 billion.

50
00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:49.240
[George] The British economist John Maynard Keynes resigned in disgust, warning prophetically that the punitive economic terms of Versailles would destroy Germany's economy and inevitably breed a second world war.

51
00:06:49.240 --> 00:07:00.920
[George] The resulting Treaty of Versailles, while creating the historic League of Nations, imposed crippling reparations and war guilt clauses upon Germany that sowed the toxic seeds of future conflict.

52
00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:12.040
[George] The compromises forced upon Wilson in Paris deeply compromised his original principles, creating an unstable international architecture that would fail to prevent future catastrophe.

53
00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:20.240
[George] Yet for all the compromises, Wilson succeeded in embedding the Covenant of the League of Nations into the very first chapter of the Treaty of Versailles.

54
00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:26.720
[George] To Wilson, the League was the divine mechanism that would rectify every injustice of the treaty over time.

55
00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:31.280
[George] The sacred core of the covenant was Article X, which declared.

56
00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:41.120
[George] The members of the League undertake to respect and preserve as against external aggression the territorial integrity and existing political independence of all members of the League.

57
00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:47.360
[George] Backslash and Backslash and Article X established the revolutionary doctrine of collective security.

58
00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.040
[George] An attack on one nation would be treated as an attack on all.

59
00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:55.360
[George] Enforced by international economic blockades and military force.

60
00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:00.880
[George] Wilson believed Article X would make aggressive territorial conquest obsolete forever.

61
00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:08.880
[George] But conservative critics in the United States, led by Republican Senator Henry Cabot Lodge, viewed Article X with utter dread.

62
00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:21.600
[George] They argued that it surrendered the constitutional warming power of the United States Congress to a foreign council in Geneva, obligating American doughys to shed their blood in obscure European boundary disputes.

63
00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:34.480
[George] Back in Washington, Wilson encountered a fierce, unyielding phalanx of Republican senators led by Henry Cabot Lodge, who feared that the League would surrender American sovereign independence to foreign control.

64
00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:44.400
[George] Lodge's reservations regarding Article X touched the foundational constitutional question of whether Congress alone possesses the sovereign authority to declare war.

65
00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:51.920
[George] In April 1919, at the height of the most intense negotiations, catastrophe struck the American president.

66
00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] Wilson collapsed in Paris with a violent fever of 103 degrees, coughing spasms, and severe abdominal cramps.

67
00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:06.440
[George] He had contracted the deadly Spanish influenza, which was killing tens of millions across the globe.

68
00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:18.040
[George] Modern medical historians have uncovered evidence that the virus caused severe neurological and vascular damage to Wilson's brain, exacerbating his pre-existing arteriosclerosis.

69
00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:25.400
[George] Backslash N backslash N when Wilson emerged from his sickbed days later, his personality was profoundly altered.

70
00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:31.080
[George] The calm, logical scholar had become paranoid, irrational, and rigidly stubborn.

71
00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:41.960
[George] He accused French servants of being German spies, obsessed over the placement of White House furniture in his Paris apartment, and became utterly incapable of diplomatic compromise.

72
00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:51.480
[George] Sensing his vulnerability, Clemenceau and Lloyd George extracted further harsh concessions from the exhausted, neurologically impaired American leader.

73
00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:56.760
[George] Wilson had won the league, but his physical and mental health were broken beyond repair.

74
00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:08.440
[George] Wilson's rigid, uncompromising moral certitude blinded him to the necessity of legislative compromise, turning a battle over international diplomacy into a bitter personal war of wills.

75
00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:18.280
[George] The tragic failure of the treaty proved that a visionary statesman must cultivate the support of legislative partners as carefully as he articulates lofty moral ideals.

76
00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:32.360
[George] On Saturday, June 28, 1919, five years to the day after the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo, the drama concluded inside the magnificent Hall of Mirrors at the Palace of Versailles.

77
00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:43.880
[George] Standing in the very room where the German Empire had been proclaimed in 1871, hundreds of Allied dignitaries, generals, and diplomats gathered under towering crystal chandeliers.

78
00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:57.200
[George] Two pale, humiliated German delegates were marched in like condemned prisoners to sign the Treaty of Versailles, backslash and backslash and Woodrow Wilson sat on the dais, signing the Leatherbound Treaty with a gold pen.

79
00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:02.320
[George] Outside, artillery batteries fired thunderous victory salutes across Paris.

80
00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:07.600
[George] Yet as the crowds cheered, Wilson looked exhausted, haunted, and grave.

81
00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:15.440
[George] He knew that the European peace was flawed and fragile, but he believed with all his heart that the League of Nations would redeem humanity.

82
00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:22.880
[George] Ahead of him lay his greatest and deadliest battle, convincing his own country to accept the treaty and ratify the League.

83
00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:29.520
[George] Wilson boarded the George Washington and steamed home toward Washington, walking directly into an ambush.

84
00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:40.080
[George] That fateful decision to bypass the Senate and take his case directly to the American people would exact an unbearable physical toll upon the exhausted, fragile president.

85
00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:49.040
[George] The defeat of the League in the Senate was the greatest heartbreak of Wilson's life, casting a tragic shadow over his final years in the White House.

86
00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:55.280
[Rebecca] Woodrow Wilson's struggle at Versailles produced a flawed piece that sowed the seeds of future conflict.

87
00:11:55.280 --> 00:12:03.280
[Rebecca] Yet his creation of the League of Nations established the modern framework of international collective security that became the United Nations.

88
00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:07.360
[Rebecca] Next week, we examine the tragic climax of Wilson's life.

89
00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:16.240
[Rebecca] The Western Train Tour, the devastating stroke in Pueblo, Colorado, and the secret 14 month presidency of First Lady Edith Wilson.

90
00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:20.400
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

